{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"8a214294-f08d-4d62-bf5e-fbfe34142c15","title":"Riemann sums: approximating area with rectangles"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"8a214294-f08d-4d62-bf5e-fbfe34142c15","step":{"id":"s1","script":"We want the area under this curve from zero to two. Rectangles will give us an estimate without needing an exact area formula.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Riemann sums"},{"op":"equation","id":"function","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f(x)=x^2,\\qquad 0\\le x\\le 2"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-0.1,2.3],"y":[-0.2,4.7],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"y"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"curve","y":"x^2","domain":[0,2],"label":"y = x²"},{"kind":"segment","id":"boundary","from":[2,0],"to":{"on":"curve","x":2},"stroke":"dashed"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"8a214294-f08d-4d62-bf5e-fbfe34142c15","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Split the interval into four equal widths. Using each right endpoint, draw a rectangle whose height matches the curve there.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"width","anchor":{"el":"function","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\Delta x=\\frac{2-0}{4}=\\frac12"},{"op":"figure","id":"rectangles","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","points":[[0,0],[0.5,0],[0.5,0.25],[0,0.25]],"closed":true},{"kind":"point","id":"sample1","at":{"on":"curve","x":0.5}},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[0.5,0],[1,0],[1,1],[0.5,1]],"closed":true},{"kind":"point","id":"sample2","at":{"on":"curve","x":1}},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[1,0],[1.5,0],[1.5,2.25],[1,2.25]],"closed":true},{"kind":"point","id":"sample3","at":{"on":"curve","x":1.5}},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[1.5,0],[2,0],[2,4],[1.5,4]],"closed":true},{"kind":"point","id":"sample4","at":{"on":"curve","x":2}}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"8a214294-f08d-4d62-bf5e-fbfe34142c15","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Each rectangle contributes width times height. Add the four areas to get three point seven five. Is this estimate too large or too small?","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"sumrule","anchor":{"el":"width","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"R_4=\\sum_{i=1}^{4}f(x_i)\\,\\Delta x"},{"op":"equation","id":"calculation","anchor":{"el":"sumrule","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"R_4=\\frac12\\left(\\frac14+1+\\frac94+4\\right)=\\frac{15}{4}=3.75"},{"op":"figure","id":"widthmark","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"measure","from":[1,0.6],"to":[1.5,0.6],"label":"½"}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Does this right-endpoint sum overestimate or underestimate the area? Why?","expected_gist":"Overestimates: the increasing curve lies below each rectangle top."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"8a214294-f08d-4d62-bf5e-fbfe34142c15","step":{"id":"s4","script":"The shaded area stays below the rectangle tops, so our estimate is too large. Using more, narrower rectangles makes the approximation more accurate.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"actualarea","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"shade","under":"curve","from":0,"to":2},{"kind":"segment","from":[1.25,2.25],"to":{"on":"curve","x":1.25},"arrow":true},{"kind":"text","at":[0.65,3.3],"text":"excess","size":"small"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"conclusion","anchor":{"el":"calculation","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"A<R_4=3.75"},{"op":"text","id":"refine","anchor":{"el":"conclusion","side":"below","gap":0.08},"content":"More rectangles → better estimate"}],"checkpoint":null}}
